{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## PREDICTION, RECORDED BEFORE THE RUN — the failure mode I expect if 1311 does not work live\n\nWritten now, deliberately, so a red result is interpretable on sight instead of rationalised\nafterwards. This epic's pattern has been to explain each live failure convincingly *after* seeing\nit; a prediction made in advance is falsifiable and cheap.\n\n### Most likely failure mode: the Epic Link is not on the issue's EDIT SCREEN\n\nJira rejects an update to a field absent from the relevant screen with **HTTP 400** and a body of the\nform:\n\n    {\"errorMessages\":[],\"errors\":{\"customfield_10014\":\"Field 'customfield_10014' cannot be set.\n     It is not on the appropriate screen, or unknown.\"}}\n\nThis is a well-known Jira behaviour and it is **screen configuration, not a code defect**. It is\nplausible here specifically because the harness's scratch project is created from whichever template\nthe DC image happens to ship (the same reason `_subtask_type_name` and `_seed_epic` discover the\nissue types rather than hardcoding them) — nothing guarantees that template puts Epic Link on the\nEdit screen of a standard issue type.\n\n**How to tell it apart from the alternatives, from the run output alone:**\n\n * **HTTP 400 naming the customfield and \"not on the appropriate screen\"** → screen configuration.\n   The code is right; the harness project needs the field added to the screen, or the cell needs to\n   add it during setup. This should be reported as SETUP FAILED, not as a rebar defect.\n * **HTTP 200 and the read-back still shows the OLD value (or null)** → accept-and-ignore, i.e. the\n   same shape as [rebar:1a9f-50c0-e7a5-4fda]. That would be the serious outcome: it would mean DC\n   silently discards parent writes across BOTH the `fields.parent` and Epic Link routes, and the\n   \"silent success\" class this epic exists to eliminate is deeper than one field.\n * **HTTP 404 on a `/rest/agile/` or `/rest/greenhopper/` path** → impossible by construction now;\n   1311 issues an ordinary `PUT /rest/api/2/issue/{key}`. If this appears, something other than\n   `set_parent` is making the call.\n * **`SETUP FAILED ... no 'Epic Link' field`** → the instance has no Jira Software epic fields at\n   all, and the transport's decline is correct; the epic-parent path simply cannot be exercised on\n   this harness.\n\n### What I expect\n\nThe Epic Link write is an ordinary custom-field update on the standard REST v2 endpoint, which is\nfar better-supported on DC than the greenhopper route that 404'd — so I expect it to SUCCEED. But\n1302 was expected to succeed too, on a docstring's authority, so this is a prediction and not a\nclaim. **AC1 is ticked off the harness or not at all.**\n\n### If the screen-configuration mode is what happens\n\nThe fix is in the CELL, not the transport: discover the Edit screen and add the field during setup,\nor fail as SETUP with the field name — the pattern `_seed_epic` already follows for the missing Epic\ntype. Do NOT respond by writing `fields.parent` as a fallback; DC no-ops it, which is the silent\nfailure the whole ticket exists to remove."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785785345020524001,"uuid":"c5a76b08-a850-46bb-96ea-7cdd229146d8"}